# Design Token Rules

Tokens live in `src/tokens/` and are the **single source of truth** for every visual value in the design system.

## Token categories

| File | What it contains |
|------|-----------------|
| `colors.ts` | Raw scales by hue — `grey`, `primary`, `blue`, `red`, `yellow`, `orange`, `purple`, `green`. The only place a hex is written |
| `surface.ts` | Background fills, per light/dark |
| `border.ts` | Outline colours, per light/dark |
| `text.ts` | Font colours, per light/dark |
| `icon.ts` | Icon colours, per light/dark |
| `typography.ts` | Font families, sizes, weights, line-heights |
| `spacing.ts` | Component spacing scale in px |
| `responsive.ts` | Page-scale type and spacing, per breakpoint |
| `radius.ts` | Border-radius values |
| `elevation.ts` | Box-shadow elevations |

## Font and icon colours are named exactly as Figma names them

`text` and `icon` keys **are** the Figma variable names. When a designer
says "make this `text/default/b2`", drop the category prefix and turn the
slashes into property access — that is the token, with nothing to
translate:

| Designer says | You write |
|---|---|
| `text/default/b2` | `text.default.b2` |
| `text/primary/3` | `text.primary[3]` |
| `icon/success/2` | `icon.success[2]` |
| `text/default/body on-color` | `text.default['body on-color']` |
| `text/disabled/on-color` | `text.disabled['on-color']` |

Never substitute a rung you think looks closer. If the name a designer
gives you does not exist, say so rather than picking a neighbour — a
wrong rung is a visible bug that looks like a deliberate choice.

Two quirks are Figma's own, not typos: `text/warning` and `text/orange`
run `0, 2, 3, 4` with no rung `1`, and `icon/warning` starts at `1` where
`text/warning` starts at `0`.

`surface` and `border` are **not** named this way — they still rename
Figma's tier numbers to state names (`border/primary/2` is
`border.primary.defaultHover`). Check `src/tokens/border.ts` rather than
guessing.

### Renamed from the 1.0.x names

Releases up to 1.0.1 gave these rungs descriptive names. Every one moved:

| 1.0.x | Now |
|---|---|
| `text.default.body` | `text.default.b1` |
| `text.default.caption` | `text.default.b2` |
| `text.default.placeholder` | `text.default.b3` |
| `text.default.headingOnColor` | `text.default['heading on-color']` |
| `text.default.bodyOnColor` | `text.default['body on-color']` |
| `text.default.captionOnColor` | `text.default['caption on-color']` |
| `text.default.placeholderOnColor` | `text.default['placeholder on-color']` |
| `text.<role>.body` | `text.<role>[1]` — `[0]` on `warning`/`orange` |
| `text.<role>.caption` | `text.<role>[2]` |
| `text.<role>.accent` | `text.<role>[3]` |
| `text.<role>.onColorHover` | `text.<role>[4]` |
| `text.disabled.onColor` | `text.disabled['on-color']` |

`heading`, `subtle` and `disabled.default` did not move. `icon` moved
identically, except that `icon.warning.body` is `icon.warning[1]`.

No token *value* changed — all 78 rungs resolve to the same light and
dark hex as before. This is a rename only.

## Adding or updating tokens

1. Update the token in `src/tokens/<category>.ts`
2. Update `src/theme/index.ts` if the token name changed (rare)
3. Run `npm run generate` to refresh MCP data
4. No component changes needed — they reference theme keys, not raw values

## Hardcoded values are NEVER allowed

Anywhere in `src/`, you must use tokens through the theme. The CI lints for this.

### ❌ Forbidden patterns

```tsx
// Hardcoded hex
<Box sx={{ color: '#3B82F6' }} />

// Hardcoded pixels
<Box sx={{ padding: '16px', marginTop: '24px' }} />

// Hardcoded font sizes
<Typography sx={{ fontSize: '14px' }} />

// Hardcoded shadows
<Box sx={{ boxShadow: '0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1)' }} />

// Hardcoded radius
<Box sx={{ borderRadius: '8px' }} />
```

### ✅ Correct patterns

```tsx
// Color via theme palette key
<Box sx={{ color: 'primary.main' }} />
<Box sx={{ color: 'error.main' }} />
<Box sx={{ color: 'text.secondary' }} />

// Spacing via theme multiplier (p: 2 = theme.spacing(2) = 16px)
<Box sx={{ p: 2, mt: 3 }} />

// Typography via variant
<Typography variant="body2" />

// Shadows via elevation
<Paper elevation={1} />

// Radius via theme.shape (default) or component-specific override
<Box sx={{ borderRadius: 1 }} />  // theme.shape.borderRadius
```

## How sx prop spacing works

MUI's spacing is **multiplier-based**, not pixel-based:

```tsx
// theme.spacing(n) returns `${n * 8}px` by default

p: 0     // 0px
p: 0.5   // 4px
p: 1     // 8px
p: 2     // 16px
p: 3     // 24px
p: 4     // 32px

// Shorthand props:
m, mt, mr, mb, ml, mx, my   // margin
p, pt, pr, pb, pl, px, py   // padding
```

## How theme palette references work

MUI's palette has predefined keys you reference as strings in `sx`:

```tsx
// Brand
'primary.main', 'primary.light', 'primary.dark', 'primary.contrastText'
'secondary.main', 'secondary.light', 'secondary.dark'

// Semantic
'error.main', 'warning.main', 'info.main', 'success.main'

// Text
'text.primary', 'text.secondary', 'text.disabled'

// Backgrounds
'background.default', 'background.paper'

// Greys
'grey.50', 'grey.100', ..., 'grey.900'

// Divider
'divider'
```

## Custom token access (when sx isn't enough)

```tsx
// In a styled component
import { styled } from '@mui/material/styles';
const Wrapper = styled('div')(({ theme }) => ({
  color: theme.palette.primary.main,
  padding: theme.spacing(2),
  borderRadius: theme.shape.borderRadius,
}));

// In a regular component with useTheme
import { useTheme } from '@mui/material/styles';
const Component = () => {
  const theme = useTheme();
  return <div style={{ color: theme.palette.primary.main }} />;
};
```

## Token deprecation

If you need to deprecate a token:

1. Mark it `@deprecated` in the source with JSDoc
2. Keep it exported for 1 minor version (so consumers can migrate)
3. Add a console warning in dev mode if it's actively used
4. Remove in the next major version
